"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,225\nEpisode 8\n\n3\n00:00:07,305 --> 00:00:09,211\nSpatial memory\n\n4\n00:00:12,011 --> 00:00:13,794\nA rat explores a box.\n\n5\n00:00:14,066 --> 00:00:18,598\nIn its hippocampus, one cell fires only when the rat is in one corner.\n\n6\n00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:21,621\nAnother fires only by the far wall.\n\n7\n00:00:21,985 --> 00:00:25,167\nTogether, these place cells build a map of the room.\n\n8\n00:00:25,609 --> 00:00:28,614\nThey were first reported in 1971,\n\n9\n00:00:28,792 --> 00:00:31,184\nand later earned a Nobel Prize.\n\n10\n00:00:31,354 --> 00:00:33,621\nNext door, in entorhinal cortex,\n\n11\n00:00:33,872 --> 00:00:36,104\ngrid cells fire at many spots,\n\n12\n00:00:36,317 --> 00:00:39,051\nlaid out in a repeating triangular pattern,\n\n13\n00:00:39,176 --> 00:00:41,214\nlike coordinates on graph paper.\n\n14\n00:00:41,785 --> 00:00:45,781\nTogether, place cells and grid cells form a positioning system,\n\n15\n00:00:46,034 --> 00:00:47,766\nan inner GPS.\n\n16\n00:00:47,936 --> 00:00:49,885\nAnd it can change in adults.\n\n17\n00:00:50,211 --> 00:00:54,047\nLondon taxi trainees spend three to four years learning the city.\n\n18\n00:00:54,492 --> 00:00:58,591\nThose who qualified gained grey matter in the back of the hippocampus.\n\n19\n00:00:58,957 --> 00:01:00,444\nThose who didn't qualify,\n\n20\n00:01:00,464 --> 00:01:03,541\nand the controls, showed no such change.\n\n21\n00:01:03,711 --> 00:01:05,840\nA memory palace borrows this map.\n\n22\n00:01:06,315 --> 00:01:09,391\nPlace each item at a spot along a familiar route,\n\n23\n00:01:09,644 --> 00:01:11,381\nthen walk the route to recall it.\n\n24\n00:01:11,867 --> 00:01:13,740\nAfter forty days of practice,\n\n25\n00:01:13,919 --> 00:01:18,515\nbeginners went from about twenty-six words out of seventy-two to sixty-two.\n\n26\n00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:21,763\nFour months later, much of the gain remained.\n\n27\n00:01:21,933 --> 00:01:24,536\nThe map may reach beyond physical space.\n\n28\n00:01:24,894 --> 00:01:28,607\nPeople learned a set of birds that varied in neck and leg length.\n\n29\n00:01:28,994 --> 00:01:31,800\nAs they moved through that abstract bird space,\n\n30\n00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:34,523\ntheir brains showed a grid-like signal.\n\n31\n00:01:34,693 --> 00:01:35,832\nTwo cautions.\n\n32\n00:01:36,151 --> 00:01:39,334\nMemory palaces are strong for lists and sequences,\n\n33\n00:01:39,371 --> 00:01:40,737\nnot for understanding.\n\n34\n00:01:41,071 --> 00:01:44,266\nAnd brain size findings come from small groups.\n\n35\n00:01:44,554 --> 00:01:46,513\nA place helps you find an idea.\n\n36\n00:01:46,763 --> 00:01:47,983\nIt doesn't explain it.\n\n37\n00:01:48,153 --> 00:01:48,982\nFor teaching.\n\n38\n00:01:49,304 --> 00:01:51,415\nGive ideas a consistent place.\n\n39\n00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:54,423\nKeep the same board layout from week to week.\n\n40\n00:01:54,754 --> 00:01:56,830\nDraw diagrams with stable positions,\n\n41\n00:01:56,850 --> 00:01:58,334\nand come back to them.\n\n42\n00:01:58,649 --> 00:02:00,427\nShow a map of the course,\n\n43\n00:02:00,447 --> 00:02:02,151\nso each topic sits somewhere.\n\n44\n00:02:02,527 --> 00:02:05,335\nAnd let students lay out what they know in space.\n\n45\n00:02:05,505 --> 00:02:07,503\nThe brain is built to remember where.\n\n46\n00:02:07,901 --> 00:02:09,324\nGive your ideas a place,\n\n47\n00:02:09,344 --> 00:02:11,045\nand they're easier to find.\n\n"
